    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This research continued an investigation into the viability of using chloride concentration \r\nto determine the mass inflow rate of feed zone fluid in a geothermal well. Specifically, \r\nchloride concentration is measured across the vertical depth of a wellbore, which can then \r\nbe used in an analytical calculation from a chemical balance to infer the feed zone inflow \r\nrate. This project holds direct application for the characterization and quantification of the \r\nartificial fractures that distinguish the wells of Enhanced Geothermal Systems. This report \r\nfocuses on laboratory experiments that replicated the fluid flow behavior in geothermal \r\nwells to test the chloride tool’s accuracy and sensitivity in quantifying the feed zone inflow \r\nrates. \r\nInitial efforts focused on modifying the Artificial Well System in the Stanford Geothermal \r\nLaboratory and calibrating the chloride tool. The modifications to the Artificial Well \r\nSystem enabled continuous freshwater experimentation and reduced possibilities of \r\nexperimental error. The calibration process determined an empirical relationship between \r\nthe voltage readings of the two ion selective electrodes (ISE) housed in the chloride tool \r\nand the chloride concentration of the surrounding fluid.  \r\nSubsequently, static experiments were conducted to assess the sensitivity of the chloride \r\ntool to key independent variables. These experiments granted insight into the effects of \r\nvarying the chloride concentration of the feed zone fluid, the vertical position of the tool \r\nrelative to the feed zone, the feed zone inflow rate, and the presence of additional chemical \r\nspecies in the feed zone fluid on the voltage readings and inferred chloride molarity. \r\nAdditionally, dynamic experiments investigated the effects of these variables, and the \r\nwellbore flow rate, on the ability of the chloride tool to locate and quantify the feed zone \r\ninflow rate.  \r\nThese experiments indicated that higher chloride concentrations, upward motion, closer \r\nproximity to the feed zone jet, and higher feed zone inflow rates resulted in more accurate \r\ncharacterization of the feed zone. Additionally, the presence of additional anions in the \r\nfeed zone fluid was shown to both increase the magnitude of and reduce the noise in the \r\nvoltage readings from the ISE probes. When additional anions were present, the absolute \r\nchloride concentration could not be determined accurately, but relative changes to apparent \r\nchloride concentration still provided accurate estimates for the quantification of the feed \r\nzone inflow rate. \r\nThe findings of this project suggest that the chloride tool could accurately characterize the \r\nmass inflow rate of the artificial geothermal feed zone using relative changes in chloride \r\nconcentration.",
